#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "8787186e-94bd-53da-9304-b0d3efbcb966" :question/math-academy-id "e-5823" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6a\\cdot a\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}})+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "84d3492d-12bb-50c2-b5b4-5f08567eb4f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5825" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x}  = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "8e0fa2cd-2b29-510a-868f-4779de8536e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6260" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{x+2}{x} = \\dfrac{x-4}{x} + 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x,$ so the least common denominator is $x.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{x} & =\\frac{x-4}{x}+2 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{x}) & =x\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{x}+2) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{x} & =x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{x}+x\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{x}}+2x \\\\ x+2 & =x-4+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =x-4+2x \\\\ x+2 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3x-x & =2+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "5d20e960-7554-5a05-b1e0-2b98b944e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-6261" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{1+x}{x} + \\dfrac{5+x}{6x} = \\dfrac{1-x}{3x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x,$ and $3x,$ so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x} & =\\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =2\\cdot 3x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{\\cancel{x}})+\\cancel{6x}\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{\\cancel{6x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3x}\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{3x}}) \\\\ 6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x) \\\\ 6+6x+5+x & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+11 & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+2x & =2-11 \\\\ 9x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2562c2a5-6799-5040-a6d8-35239d94c6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2562c2a5-6799-5040-a6d8-35239d94c6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293095} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb132b1f-eced-50c2-9b8d-4aadb8c3a8b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ce28150-db62-5cfe-975f-e10652804e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-276864" :question/problem "Solve for $f$ where $\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}=\\frac{f+2}{2f}.$\n\n$f=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $f=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $f,\\,2f,$ and $5f.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(f,2f,5f)=10f.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10f,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =\\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10f(\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}) & =10f(\\frac{f+2}{2f}) \\\\ 10f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-10f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =10f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-2\\cdot 5f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =5\\cdot 2f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{f}\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{\\cancel{f}}-2\\cdot \\cancel{5f}\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{\\cancel{5f}} & =5\\cdot \\cancel{2f}\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{\\cancel{2f}} \\\\ 10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $f,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2) \\\\ 10f+60-6f-18 & =5f+10 \\\\ 4f+42 & =5f+10 \\\\ -f & =-32 \\\\ f & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $f=0$ as a solution. But since $f=32\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $f=32.$"}]}
